<div class="lc"><div class="lc-aim"><strong>By the end of this lesson you can:</strong><ul><li>describe upward, downward and stable trends with accurate verbs, nouns and adverbs</li><li>paraphrase the question in your introduction without changing its meaning</li><li>avoid the most common Task 1 vocabulary mistakes</li></ul></div><h5>Verbs and nouns for change</h5>
<table><thead><tr><th>Direction</th><th>Verb</th><th>Noun</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Up</td><td>rise, increase, grow, climb, go up</td><td>a rise, an increase, growth, a climb</td></tr><tr><td>Down</td><td>fall, decrease, decline, drop, go down</td><td>a fall, a decrease, a decline, a drop</td></tr><tr><td>Stay the same</td><td>remain stable, stay constant, level off</td><td>a plateau</td></tr><tr><td>Up then down</td><td>peak at ... then fall</td><td>a peak</td></tr><tr><td>Down then up</td><td>hit a low of ... then recover</td><td>a low point / a trough</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h5>Adjectives and adverbs for size and speed</h5>
<table><thead><tr><th>How big / how fast</th><th>Adjective + noun</th><th>Verb + adverb</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Big or fast</td><td>a sharp rise, a dramatic fall, a significant increase</td><td>rose sharply, fell dramatically, increased significantly</td></tr><tr><td>Small or slow</td><td>a slight rise, a gradual decline</td><td>rose slightly, declined gradually</td></tr><tr><td>Steady</td><td>a steady increase</td><td>increased steadily</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Use the two patterns to vary your sentences:</p>
<ul><li>Verb + adverb: <em>Sales <strong>rose sharply</strong> in 2015.</em></li><li>There was + adjective + noun: <em>There was <strong>a sharp rise</strong> in sales in 2015.</em></li></ul>
<div class="lc-warn"><strong>Watch out.</strong> <strong>Rise</strong> and <strong>fall</strong> have no object ("sales rose", never "sales rose the price"). <strong>Increase</strong> and <strong>decrease</strong> can be both: <em>The company increased prices.</em></div>
<h5>Prepositions with numbers</h5>
<table><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>to</strong> = where it ended</td><td>rose <strong>to</strong> 40%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>by</strong> = the size of the change</td><td>rose <strong>by</strong> 10%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>from ... to</strong></td><td>rose <strong>from</strong> 30% <strong>to</strong> 40%</td></tr><tr><td><strong>at</strong> = a level or a peak</td><td>peaked <strong>at</strong> 40%, stood <strong>at</strong> 40%</td></tr></tbody></table>
<h5>Paraphrasing the question</h5>
<p>Never copy the question into your introduction. Change the vocabulary and, where you can, the structure. Keep the meaning identical.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Original</th><th>Paraphrase</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The graph shows</td><td>The line graph illustrates / gives information about / compares</td></tr><tr><td>the number of</td><td>the figures for / the amount of / how many</td></tr><tr><td>between 2000 and 2010</td><td>over a ten-year period / from 2000 to 2010</td></tr><tr><td>in three countries</td><td>in three different nations</td></tr><tr><td>percentage</td><td>proportion / share</td></tr></tbody></table>
<div class="lc-ex"><div class="lc-ex-t">Question and paraphrase</div><p><em>The graph below shows the percentage of households with internet access in three countries between 2000 and 2020.</em></p><p><strong>Paraphrase:</strong> The line graph compares the proportion of homes that were online in three nations over a twenty-year period.</p></div>
<div class="lc-try"><div class="lc-try-t">Try it</div><p>Paraphrase each sentence.</p><ol><li>The chart shows the number of visitors to four museums in 2019.</li><li>The table gives information about the average price of houses in five cities.</li><li>The graph illustrates changes in the percentage of people who smoke.</li></ol><details><summary>Show suggested answers</summary><ol><li>The bar chart compares how many people went to four museums in 2019.</li><li>The table compares what a typical home cost in five cities.</li><li>The line graph shows how the proportion of smokers changed over time.</li></ol></details></div>
<div class="lc-take"><strong>Take away.</strong> Pick the verb, noun and adverb that match the size and speed of the change, get your prepositions exactly right, and change the question's words so the examiner sees your own vocabulary.</div></div>
